      "profile": "SP-05",
      "question": "One duct in a services IFC4 file I opened has an odd notch taken out of it, and I can't tell whether that's an opening or some other cut. Where can I check what is cutting into it, and can I take the cut off if it shouldn't be there?",
      "reference": {
        "answer": {
          "support": ["C2.Q1", "C3.Q1", "C3.Q2", "C4.Q1", "C4.Q2", "C3.Q3", "C4.Q3"],
          "text": "Look in the Geometric Relationships panel, which shows how the current IFC element relates geometrically to other elements. Its Voids section lists the voids (openings) associated with the element, each with an associated element, and its Booleans section shows the boolean operations applied to the element's geometry, including how many are manual (user-defined) versus automatic. In both sections the \"x\" button removes the void or the selected boolean operation."
        },
        "assumptions": ["blender", "bonsai", "model_state"],
        "hazard": null,
        "scope_note": null,
        "steps": [
          {
            "support": ["C1.Q1"],
            "text": "With the duct selected, go to the Property Editor's Geometry and Materials sub-tab."
          },
          {
            "support": ["C2.Q1"],
            "text": "Expand the Geometric Relationships panel."
          },
          {
            "support": ["C3.Q1", "C3.Q2"],
            "text": "Read the Voids section for openings, each listed with its associated element."
          },
          {
            "support": ["C4.Q1", "C4.Q2"],
            "text": "Read the Booleans section for boolean operations and the count of manual (user-defined) versus automatic ones."
          },
          {
            "support": ["C3.Q3", "C4.Q3"],
            "text": "If a cut should not be there, use its \"x\" button: on a void it removes the void from the element, and in Booleans it removes the selected boolean operation."
          }
        ],
